### 3.2. Notify NM on commit to trigger replication

At the end of `commit_write_session_ss()`, after successful merge and before returning:

```c
````c
// filepath: /home/shrawani/course-project-shellshocked/ss.c
int commit_write_session_ss(const char *filename, const char *username, int sent_idx) {
    // ...existing merge + file write logic...

    free_file_content(main_fc);
    free_file_content(temp_fc);

    // Clean up write session
    remove_write_session(filename, username, sent_idx);
    printf("Completed one write op!\n");
    log_formatted(LOG_INFO, "Committed write session for %s by %s", filename, username);

    // NEW: notify NM so it can replicate this write asynchronously
    Message notify;
    init_message(&notify);
    notify.type = MSG_COMMIT_WRITE;
    strncpy(notify.filename, filename, MAX_FILENAME - 1);
    strncpy(notify.sender, username, MAX_USERNAME - 1);
    notify.sentence_index = sent_idx;
    // word_index is not known at commit; set to -1, and NM will treat as "whole sentence changed"
    notify.word_index = -1;
    // data omitted here for large writes; replicas will be eventually consistent via sentences.
    // For finer-grained replication you can send last word or entire sentence text.
    int rc = send_message(ss.nm_sock, &notify);
    if (rc < 0) {
        log_formatted(LOG_WARNING, "Failed to notify NM about commit for %s", filename);
    }

    return SUCCESS;
}
`````

This keeps client semantics unchanged; replication is best-effort after commit.

